hey this is pressure Walker in a barn  100 chicks sit peacefully in a circle  suddenly each chick randomly pecks the  chick immediately to its left or right  what is the expected number of unpacked  chicks this problem was asked today in  the 2017 Raytheon national math counts  competition as reported in the New York  Times a 13 year old buzzed in in under a  second and got the correct answer to win  a $20,000 scholarship when I was in  grade school I have done math towns  competitions and I was never that good  at solving these problems  congratulations to the winner in this  video I'm going to ask you can you  figure out what the answer to this  problem is give this problem a try and  when you're ready keep watching the  video for the solution the answer is 25  chicks intuitively a chick is not pecked  if it only if the chick to its left  pecks left and the chip to its right  pecks right I will illustrate this in a  simple example of four chicks around a  circle focus on the chick in the bottom  of the circle in order that this chick  is not packed  we need the chick to its left to peck  left and we need the chick to it's right  to peck right what is the probability of  this happening assuming each chick pecks  in a direction with a 1/2 chance there's  a 1/2 chance the left chick text left  and 1/2 chance the right chick pecks  right thus there is a 1/2 times 1/2  which equals 1/4 chance that a chick is  not pecked as this is true for every  single chick we expect 1/4 of the chicks  not  detect meaning there will be 100 times  1/4 which is equal to 25 unpacked chicks  so that's the answer as written in the  competition however I'm going to be  pedantic and say the question is  technically unsolvable as stated the  details aren't important look again at  the wording of the question it is stated  that each chick randomly pecks the chick  immediately to its left or right but  what does randomly pecks mean it's  actually a vague term a chick that pecks  left with a 1% chance and right with a  99% chance also randomly pecks to its  left or right the problem should have  stated randomly pecks to each direction  with equal chance furthermore we also to  know whether each chick randomly texts  using the same probability distribution  one chick might be flipping a coin which  means a 50% chance to peck left to right  but another chick might be rolling a  dice for a single number which means a  1/6 chance to peck left and 5/6 chance  to peck right for the purpose of this  problem we'll assume that each chick is  using the same probability distribution  but even with that caveat we still have  the issue that we need to know the  probability that each chick pecks left  or right and the exact probability will  affect the ultimate answer as I'll  explain in the next few slides so let's  solve the problem formally if you've  been watching my videos you should know  how to solve this the problem is very  much like the video about guessing on a  matching test or figuring out how many  shuffle cards do not move in a random  shuffle well number the checks from 1 to  100 and index them by the variable I  will define a random variable X sub I to  equal 0 if the chick is pecked and 1 if  the chick is unpacked let's define the  probability a chick pecks left to equal  P and the probability a chick pecks  right  two equal one minus P these are  complementary events because we know the  chick is pecking in some direction we  now compute the expected value of x sub  I by definition this is equal to zero  times the probability of check respect  and one times the probability a chick is  unpacked this simplifies to be the  probability a chick is unpacked as  stated before this depends on the  probabilities that the chick on the Left  pecks left and the probability the chick  on the right X right this will be equal  to P times 1 minus P we now need to  figure out how many chicks are expected  to be unpacked this will be the expected  value of the sum of 